 x_n^{k_n}$ defined?","options":["It is $a+k_1+\\cdots+k_n$.","It is the largest value among $a,k_1,\\ldots,k_n$.","It is $k_1+\\cdots+k_n$.","It is the number of nonzero variables in the input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3550edb5c36042f73374da1f"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the total degree of $f(x,y) = -2x^2y$?","options":["$1$","$3$","$2$","$-2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ebf458943b48f2f0122c97b"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression is a monomial on $\\mathbb{R}^2$?","options":["$3x^2y^0$","$x^{-1}y$","$x+y$","$\\sin(x)y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f895595afd1ff8c76e4e99b9"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What happens to the exponents when two monomials on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are multiplied?","options":["They multiply componentwise.","They are replaced by their maximum values.","They add componentwise.","They must all become $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b5ae1320a5986d21377f3500"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement about a monomial and homogeneity is supported by the definition?","options":["A monomial is homogeneous only if every exponent is $1$.","A monomial is never homogeneous unless the coefficient is positive.","Every monomial is homogeneous, with degree equal to its total degree.","Homogeneity depends only on the number of variables, not on the exponents."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9df60d78555404077f5360d4"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x,y) 